// ===========================================================================
// Statistics metrics.
//
// A metric is "how many tuples does relation R hold?", written once per metric
// in statistics-simple.dl as
//
//   NewMetricMacro("8.0", "call graph edges (INS)", CallGraphEdge(_, _)).
//
// The macro expands to Souffle's body count, which both engines accept since
// flowlog-compiler 0.8.0. One difference remains: FlowLog counts the distinct
// bindings of the NAMED variables inside the braces, so "R(_, _)" would count
// as 1. FlowLogTransformer (Doop's Groovy sources) therefore names every "_"
// of a lone-atom count/sum body on the PREPROCESSED program, where it can see
// the atom's arity, turning the expansion above into
//
//   Stats_Metrics("8.0", "call graph edges (INS)", c) :- c = count : { R(v1, v2) }.
//
// A metric over a NULLARY relation has no column to name, and an aggregate
// whose body binds no variable does not compile on 0.8.0, so the transformer
// rewrites that one case into two facts the FLOWLOG_ENGINE branch below
// completes:
//
//   NewMetricMacro("26.10", "obfuscation (boolean)", mainAnalysis.ObfuscationOn())
//
// becomes
//
//   MetricDecl("26.10", "obfuscation (boolean)").
//   _MetricOn("26.10", "obfuscation (boolean)") :- mainAnalysis.ObfuscationOn().
//
// MetricDecl records that the metric EXISTS, so the zero rule below can report 0
// when the relation is empty, as Souffle does. The zero rule keeps MetricDecl as
// a positive atom on purpose: a body that is nothing but one negated nullary
// atom is a planner internal error in flowlog-compiler (still present in 0.8.0).
// The MetricDecl and _MetricOn names and shapes are a contract between this
// file and the transformer.
//
// Mixing these plain rules with the aggregate rules of Stats_Metrics is fine
// since 0.8.0 (upstream #427); up to 0.7.0 that mix did not compile, which is
// what the per-metric _MAgg_<n> relations this file used to declare worked
// around.
//
// statistics-simple.dl itself is deliberately left untouched by all of this. It
// stays the Souffle baseline that FlowLog results are compared against.
// ===========================================================================

// The macro itself is the same for both engines: FlowLogTransformer needs this
// exact expansion as its input, and keeping one definition means the Souffle
// baseline cannot drift from what FlowLog is derived from.
#define NewMetricMacro(order, msg, body) \
  Stats_Metrics(order, msg, c) :- \
    c = count : { body }

#ifdef FLOWLOG_ENGINE

// The nullary metrics that exist in this configuration, one fact per rewritten
// rule, so it holds exactly the metrics their surrounding #ifdefs left enabled.
.decl MetricDecl(order:symbol, msg:symbol)

// A nullary metric whose relation holds its (only) tuple.
.decl _MetricOn(order:symbol, msg:symbol)

Stats_Metrics(?order, ?msg, 1) :- _MetricOn(?order, ?msg).
Stats_Metrics(?order, ?msg, 0) :- MetricDecl(?order, ?msg), !_MetricOn(?order, ?msg).

#endif // FLOWLOG_ENGINE
